umsamples}$ to a latent representation $\mathrm{enc}(x) \in \mathbb{R}^{
umtokens	imes\dims}$ by cascading residual convolutional blocks that interleave dilated~[oord2016wavenet] and strided convolutions along with ELU~[elu] non-linearities and Weight Normalization~[weightnorm]. All convolutions are causal, such that this autoencoder can run in a streaming fashion. With 4 convolutional blocks and respective striding factors $(4, 5, 6, 8)$, and a final 1D convolution with stride 2, Mimi 's encoder projects a 24kHz waveform to a latent representation of $12.5$ frames per second and dimension $D = 512$. Symmetrically, the decoder adopts a similar structure but with transposed convolutions rather than strided ones, to project the latent representation back to 24kHz audio. We discretize the latent space with a Residual Vector Quantizer~[soundstream], which iteratively applies vector quantization (VQ) to the residuals of the previous quantizer. With 
umquantizers{} quantizers, each with a codebook of \cardaudio{} centroids, the RVQ discretizes the latent space into $\{1,\dots,\cardaudio\}^{
umtokens	imes
umquantizers}$. As a baseline, we train this model with a combination of reconstruction and adversarial losses, following the setup of Encodec~[encodec]. We detail below the main changes of Mimi with respect to this default configuration. \paragraph{Transformer-based bottleneck.} \looseness=-1 To improve the ability of Mimi to encode speech into compact representations while reconstructing high-quality audio, we add Transformer modules in the bottleneck, one right before quantization and one after. These Transformers have 8 layers, 8 heads, RoPE position encodings, a finite context of 250 frames (20 seconds), GELU~[gelu] activations, a model dimension of 512 and an MLP dimension of 2048. To stabilize training, we use LayerScale~[layerscale], with initialization of the diagonal values at $0.01$. Both Transformers use causal masking, which preserves the compatibility of the whole architecture with streaming inference. Both Transformers prove to be useful with regard to perceived audio quality, while Transformer in the encoder also improves the distillation of semantic information described below (see \hyperref[tab:mimi_ablations]{Table~[tab:mimi_ablations]} for ablation studies). \looseness=-1 \paragraph{Causality and streaming.} With the mentioned hyper-parameters, Mimi is causal and can be used in a streaming fashion, both for encoding and decoding. Both its initial frame size and overall stride correspond to 80ms, meaning that given a first audio frame of 80ms, Mimi outputs a first latent timestep, which can be decoded to 80ms of output audio. \paragraph{Optimization.} \looseness=-1 Unlike purely convolutional codecs that use Adam~[adam], the introduction of Transformers into the architecture requires additional regularization with weight decay along with using the AdamW~[adamw] optimizer. More precisely, we apply weight decay only to the parameters of the Transformers, with a weight of $5\cdot 10^{-2}$. We use a learning rate of $8\cdot 10^{-4}$, a momentum decay of $0.5$ and a decay of the squared gradient of $0.9$, and an exponential moving average of weights with a decay of $0.99$. We train with a batch size of $128$ on random windows of $\SI{12}{\second}$, for 4M steps, while the context of Transformers is limited to 10s (250 frames before the last downsampling layer of the encoder, and symmetrically for the decoder). \paragraph{Quantization rate.} \looseness=-1 We use $
